.TH KINTSUGI-SCHEDULE 1 "2026-10-26" "kintsugi 0.8.0" "Kintsugi Manual"
.SH NAME
kintsugi-schedule \- scheduled one-shot and recurring jobs from a cron expression
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kintsugi schedule
add "\fIcron\fR" "\fItask\fR"
.br
.B kintsugi schedule
list
.br
.B kintsugi schedule
trigger \fIid\fR
.br
.B kintsugi schedule
pause | resume | remove \fIid\fR
.br
.B kintsugi schedule
run
.SH DESCRIPTION
Store a cron expression and a task, and fire the task when the expression comes
due. Jobs live in \fB.kintsugi/schedules.json\fR in the workspace, written
atomically at 0600.
.PP
The five cron fields are minute, hour, day-of-month, month and day-of-week. Each
supports \fB*\fR, a number, a range (\fBa-b\fR), a step (\fB*/n\fR or
\fBa-b/n\fR) and comma-separated lists; month and day-of-week also accept their
three-letter names. The shorthands \fB@hourly @daily @midnight @weekly
@monthly\fR are accepted. Anything else is rejected with the reason \- a
schedule that silently never fires is worse than a refusal.
.PP
\fBrun\fR is the foreground scheduler: it fires due jobs and waits for the next
window until interrupted. A firing is a background run in the session hub, so it
shows in the TUI roster and can be killed there. Delivery is local: the answer is
printed, and one terminal notification is raised when a backend is configured in
\fB[notify]\fR.
.SH COMMANDS
.IP \fBadd\fR "\fIcron\fR" "\fItask\fR"
Store a job. Both the cron expression and the injection guard are checked here
.IP \fBlist\fR
Jobs with state, next firing, last firing and the last error
.IP \fBtrigger\fR \fIid\fR
Fire one job now. The cron is untouched; the next window is recomputed from it
.IP \fBpause\fR \fIid\fR
Stop the job firing. The job and its window are kept
.IP \fBresume\fR \fIid\fR
Start it firing again
.IP \fBremove\fR \fIid\fR
Delete the job
.IP \fBrun\fR
Foreground scheduler until ctrl-c
.SH OPTIONS
.IP \fB\-\-every\fR 30s
For \fBrun\fR: how often to look for due jobs
.IP \fB\-\-model\fR
Provider/model to use
.IP \fB\-\-role\fR
Model role: default|coder|researcher|opsec
.SH INJECTION SAFETY
A scheduled prompt is data, never authority. It is refused when it is added, again
when it fires, and again inside the runner before the prompt is built, so a
hand-edited jobs file cannot smuggle one in. A payload that tries to grant a
permission, widen the declared scope, or write authorization or scope context is
rejected; the refusal is logged as a class and a rule label, never as the text
that tried. A scheduled turn may not change the permission matrix, the scope, the
sandbox posture, the attestation, or a hook: none of those are read from task
text anywhere in the harness.
.SH EXAMPLES
Ask for a nightly summary:
.PP
  $ kintsugi schedule add "0 3 * * *" "summarise yesterday's commits into NOTES.md"
.PP
List what is scheduled:
.PP
  $ kintsugi schedule list
.PP
Test a job without waiting for its window:
.PP
  $ kintsugi schedule trigger job-1
.PP
Run the scheduler in a terminal:
.PP
  $ kintsugi schedule run --every 30s
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kintsugi (1),
.BR kintsugi-loop (1),
.BR kintsugi-run (1)